# ── Secrets ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# .env holds real API keys. It must never be committed. .env.example is the
# safe, committed template — it documents every variable with placeholders.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.key
*.pem
service-account*.json
credentials*.json

# ── Python ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
*.egg-info/

# ── Pipeline working files ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Everything in data/ is produced by `python pipeline.py`. It is large
# (data/raw alone is ~400 MB of cached pages and PDFs) and fully reproducible,
# so none of it belongs in the repo. The one output anybody actually needs —
# the catalogue the bot serves — is committed under deliverables/dataset/.
data/

# ── Generated handoff folder ────────────────────────────────────────────
# `src/make_deliverables.py` deletes and rebuilds deliverables/ from scratch,
# so committing it wholesale would mean committing build output that changes
# on every run. Three things are kept because they are small, stable and worth
# reading without running anything:
#
#   dataset/bot_matching.json  the 219 scholarships the bot serves — also what
#                              the Dockerfile copies into the image
#   compliance/                the robots.txt policy and every URL we refused
#   reports/                   the coverage summary
deliverables/*
!deliverables/dataset/
deliverables/dataset/*
!deliverables/dataset/bot_matching.json
!deliverables/compliance/
!deliverables/reports/

# ── Business documents ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The PRD and pitch deck are Barfi Institute's, not part of the software.
# They stay on disk; they do not go into a public repository.
Documents/

# ── A different project that happens to live in this folder ─────────────
# barfi-site/ is the bebarfi.com website: 7.6 MB, its own build, no shared
# code with the bot. It belongs in its own repository. Ignored here so that
# `git add -A` cannot sweep it — and the personal CV inside it — into a
# PUBLIC repo, which is exactly what happened once.
barfi-site/

# ── Personal documents, wherever they turn up ───────────────────────────
# Defence in depth. The rules above depend on a file staying in the folder
# someone first put it in; these do not. A CV in a public repo is not a
# mistake you can take back, because the clone already happened.
*[Rr]esume*.pdf
*[Cc][Vv].pdf
*.docx
*.pptx
*.xlsx

# ── Local tool settings ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
.claude/

# ── OS / editor ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
